    ['people', 'edward-ntim-gasu', ['full_name' => 'Edward Ntim Gasu', 'slug' => 'edward-ntim-gasu', 'role' => 'staff', 'title' => 'Scientific Programmer', 'affiliation' => 'EAIFR', 'photo' => '/uploads/people/edward-ntim-gasu.png', 'bio' => "[ed-ntim.github.io](https://ed-ntim.github.io)  \nOffice: Einstein Building, Level 4, Room 8\n\n## Research interests\n\n**Scientific Computing Technology.** Exploration of emerging technologies and computing paradigms in large-scale scientific computing. Active literature review to remain current with advancements in computational methodologies (software) and hardware relevant to scientific research.\n\n**High-Performance Computing (HPC) Algorithm Development.** Development and integration of efficient algorithms to enhance throughput in the generation and post-processing of large-scale datasets. Focused on adapting and combining computational strategies to meet the demands of HPC environments.\n\n**Mathematical Modeling and Machine Learning.** Application and development of mathematical models to interpret, predict, and guide physical, biological and chemical phenomena. Models support computational analysis across various research areas encompassing chemophysical and geophysical system dynamics.\n\n**Chemical Physics of Biological Systems.** Investigation of dynamic molecular interactions within biological systems to understand their physicochemical behavior. Current focus includes studying the behavior of water under varying conditions such as the presence of salts, hydrophilic/hydrophobic interfaces, and external electric potentials.\n\n## Current projects\n\nUnderstanding the collective burst mechanism on a large scale with an optimized toolkit for automated detection of angular jumps and hydrogen bonding dynamics in liquid water.\n\n## Biography\n\nEdward Ntim Gasu obtained his BSc. and Ph.D. in Chemistry in 2016 and 2023 respectively, at the Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology, Kumasi - Ghana.\n\nIn December 2024, he earned a Specialist degree (Professional Master) in High Performance Computing through the [MHPC programme](https://www.mhpc.it), jointly run by ICTP and SISSA in Trieste, Italy.\n\nAt ICTP-EAIFR, Edward contributes to interdisciplinary research involving the use of an HPC approach. This includes, research in Scientific Computing, Mathematical Modeling, Condensed Matter Physics, Computational Biophysics, Physics of the Solid Earth, with additional responsibilities in areas of Information Technology and Cyber security asset management.", 'publications_body' => "1. **[Computational Studies Provide a Molecular Basis for the Quorum Sensing Inhibitory Action of Compounds from Dioon spinulosum Dyer Ex Eichler](https://doi.org/10.1002/slct.202203773)**  \n   Jehoshaphat Oppong Mensah, Aaron Boakye, Prince Manu, Prisca Baah Nketia, Edward Ntim Gasu, Isaac Asiamah, Lawrence Sheringham Borquaye  \n   *2023 - ChemistrySelect, 8(1), e202203773*\n\n2. **[Molecular insights into the differential membrane targeting of maximin 1 in prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells](https://doi.org/10.1080/07391102.2023.2292297)**  \n   Jehoshaphat Oppong Mensah, Aaron Boakye, Michael Laryea, Edward Ntim Gasu and Lawrence Sheringham Borquaye  \n   *2023 - Journal of Biomolecular Structure and Dynamics, 43(3), 1579–1592*\n\n3. **[Allosteric Modulation of the Main Protease (MPro) of SARS-CoV-2 by Casticin—Insights from Molecular Dynamics Simulations](https://doi.org/10.1007/s42250-022-00411-7)**  \n   Jehoshaphat Oppong Mensah, Gilbert Boadu Ampomah, Edward Ntim Gasu, Abigail Kusiwaa Adomako, Elliott Sarpong Menkah, and Lawrence Sheringham Borquaye  \n   *2022 - Chemistry Africa 5, 1305–1320*\n\n4. **[Computer-aided design of proline-rich antimicrobial peptides based on the chemophysical properties of a peptide isolated from Olivancillaria hiatula](https://doi.org/10.1080/07391102.2022.2131626)**  \n   Edward Ntim Gasu, John Kenneth Mensah, and Lawrence Sheringham Borquaye  \n   *2022 - Journal of Biomolecular Structure and Dynamics, 41(17), 8254–8275*"]],
    ['people', 'daniel-manu-marfo', ['full_name' => 'Daniel Manu-Marfo', 'slug' => 'daniel-manu-marfo', 'role' => 'faculty', 'title' => 'Senior Lecturer', 'affiliation' => 'EAIFR', 'area_slug' => 'geophysics', 'photo' => '/uploads/people/daniel-manu-marfo.jpg', 'bio' => "## Research interests\n\nThe analysis of seismological observations is paramount to the study of the Earth's interior structure as it offers the best information about the dynamic processes within the Earth. My research focus is on understanding the structure and active deformation in the Central Mediterranean region. Here, the deformation is complex and cannot be easily related to the motion of the African and European plates. The geodynamic complexity of the Central Mediterranean makes the kinematics of the present-day deformation and its relations with intraplate earthquakes less well understood. My goal is to provide new insights about the lithospheric velocity structure through high-resolution seismic tomography studies that shed light about the tectonics and deformation in the Central Mediterranean region.\n\n## Biography\n\nI received my PhD in Earth Sciences from the University of Trieste, Italy in 2020. Since then, I have been working at the Abdus Salam International Centre for Theoretical Physics until October 2024. From January 2025, I started my role here at ICTP-EAIFR as a Senior Lecturer.", 'publications_body' => "1. **[Crust–Uppermost Mantle Structure beneath the Caribbean Region from Seismic Ambient Noise Tomography](https://doi.org/10.1785/0120220062)**  \n   Bladimir Moreno Toiran, Abdelkrim Aoudia, Daniel Manu-Marfo, Radia Kherchouche, Surya Pachhai  \n   *2023 - Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 113 (3), 1064–1076*\n\n2. **[The Main Himalayan Thrust beneath Nepal and Southern Tibet illuminated by seismic ambient noise and teleseismic P wave coda autocorrelation](https://doi.org/10.1029/2022JB026195)**  \n   Hari Ram Thapa, Surya Pachhai, Abdelkrim Aoudia, Daniel Manu-Marfo, Keith Priestley, Supriyo Mitra  \n   *2023 - Journal of Geophysical Research: Solid Earth, 128*\n\n3. **[Approximate analytical expressions of the fundamental peak frequency and the amplification factor of S-wave transfer function in a viscoelastic layered model](https://doi.org/10.1007/S00024-018-2064-X)**  \n   Tran Thanh Tuan, Pham Chi Vinh, Abdelkrim Aoudia, Truong Thi Thuy Dung, Daniel Manu-Marfo  \n   *2019 - Pure Appl. Geophys. 176, 1433–1443*\n\n4. **[3D shear wave velocity model of the crust and uppermost mantle beneath the Tyrrhenian basin and margins](https://doi.org/10.1038/S41598-019-40510-Z)**  \n   D Manu-Marfo, A Aoudia, S Pachhai, R Kherchouche  \n   *2019 - Sci Rep 9, 3609*"]],
['people', 'catherine-a-meriaux', ['full_name' => 'Catherine A. Meriaux', 'slug' => 'catherine-a-meriaux', 'role' => 'director', 'title' => 'Professor of Geophysics; Director', 'affiliation' => 'EAIFR', 'area_slug' => 'geophysics', 'photo' => '/uploads/people/catherine-a-meriaux.jpg', 'bio' => "[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=bZHZupUAAAAJ&hl=en&oi=ao)  \nOffice: Einstein Building, Third Floor\n\n## Research interests\n\nHer research is motivated by her interest in the dynamics of the Earth. In this vast field of research, she has focused her work on geophysical flows driven by gravity or buoyancy. Her research portfolio includes studies on: the propagation of magma through dykes, the spreading of lava domes, granular flows, mantle convection and plate tectonics, as well as turbulent particle flows. Her research works rely on scaled physical modelling using theoretical, numerical and/or laboratory approaches. Her overriding goal is to better understand the physical processes at stake and explain the geophysical observations.\n\nResearcherID: [B-1298-2011](https://www.webofscience.com/wos/author/record/B-1298-2011)  \nORCID: [0000-0003-1683-8572](https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1683-8572)\n\n## Current projects\n\n- **[E-DOOR](https://e-doors.org)**, IGCP UNESCO, IGCP 767 project (2023–2026). Environmental Dynamics of Western Rwanda.\n- **[WALL](https://www.wallatrwanda.org/)**, NCST (Rwanda) and Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation, NCST-NRIF/GCR-CH/13/04/2023 (2024–2026). Evolution of the volcanic environment in western Rwanda with climate change: impact on health and mitigation.\n- **E-DOORs**, JEAI, IRD, France (2025–2027). Environmental dynamics of the volcanic region of Western Rwanda: An integrated study.\n- **KIGASA**, FEF-R (2025–2026). Lake Kivu and its Gases: from the Subsurface to the Atmosphere.\n- **E-LIEU**, UKRI (2026–2028). A unique Energy resource in the face of naturaL rIsks: LakE KivU (Rwanda).\n\n## Biography\n\nC.A. Meriaux has a PhD in Geophysics awarded by the University Paris VII (France) in April 1996. Since then, she has worked at the Institute of Earth Sciences at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem (IL), the Institute of Theoretical Geophysics at the University of Cambridge (UK), the Research School of Earth Sciences at the Australian National University (AU), the School of Mathematical Sciences at Monash University (AU), the Institute Dom Luiz at the University of Lisbon (PT), the School of Physics and Astronomy at Monash University (AU) and the School of Earth, Atmosphere and Environment at Monash University (AU). She started her position as a professor of Geophysics at ICTP-EAIFR in October 2021.", 'email' => "cmeriaux@eaifr.org", 'publications_body' => "1. **[From magma to macrophage: the grand challenges of volcanic environments](https://doi.org/10.1007/S00445-026-01936-2)**  \n   Catherine A. Mériaux, Edward Ntim Gasu, Guillaume Nyagatare, Vicky Ruganzu, Philbert Ntivuguruzwa, Mwumvaneza Mutagoma, Atalay Ayele, Julyan H. E. Cartwright, Valérie Cayol, Laurent Charlet, Claudia Corradino, Antonio Costa, François Darchambeau, Timothy Davis, Arnau Folch, Benjamin Gilbert, Alexandra Tiaré Gourlan, Andrew J. Hogg, Derek Keir, Silvia Massaro, Tamsin A. Mather, Clive Oppenheimer, Carolina Pagli, Virginie Pinel, Eleonora Rivalta, Alison Rust, Roberto Sulpizio, Latifa Sarra Kazi Tani, Andrew W. Woods & Davide Zanchettin  \n   *2026 - Bulletin of Volcanology (2026) 88:22*\n\n2. **[Challenges and needs of African geoscience research](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-025-01808-y;)**  \n   Asrat, A., Keane, C., A. Meriaux, C. et al.  \n   *2025 - Nat. Geosci. (2025). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41561-025-01808-y*\n\n3. **[Subsurface lateral magma propagation from Nyiragongo volcano in the Western Rift Zone of the East African Rift](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.JAFREARSCI.2025.105569)**  \n   Virginie Pinel and Catherine A. Meriaux  \n   *2025 - J. African Earth Sciences, 226,105569*\n\n4. **[All scales must be considered to understand rifts](https://doi.org/10.1038/S43017-023-00408-X)**  \n   Ebinger, C., van Wijk, J., Olaka, L., Mériaux, C.A. , and Fontijn, K.  \n   *2023 - Nature Reviews Earth & Environment, 1-2*\n\n5. **[An improved method for estimating the dissipation rate of TKE using structure functions evaluated from the motion of finite-sized neutrally-buoyant particles](https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0148473)**  \n   M. A. C. Teixeira, and Mériaux, C. A.  \n   *2023 - Phys. Fluids, 35, 065102*\n\n6. **[Estimating the filtering of turbulence properties by finite-sized particles using analytical energy spectra](https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0084622)**  \n   M. A. C. Teixeira and Meriaux, C. A.  \n   *2022 - Phys. Fluids, 34 (4), 045117*\n\n7. **[The impact of vent geometry on the growth of lava domes](https://doi.org/10.1093/GJI/GGAC005)**  \n   Meriaux, C. A., D. A. May and C. Jaupart  \n   *2022 - Geophys. J. Int., 229 (3), 1680-1694*\n\n8. **[Mantle plume dynamics at the rear of a retreating slab](https://doi.org/10.1093/GJI/GGAA222)**  \n   Meriaux, C. A., D. A. May, J. Mansour and O. Kaluza  \n   *2020 - Geophys. J. Int., 222, 1146-1163.*\n\n9. **[Dispersion of finite-size particles probing inhomogeneous and anisotropic turbulence](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.EUROMECHFLU.2020.05.015)**  \n   Meriaux, C. A., M. A. C. Teixeira, J. J. Monaghan, R. Cohen and P. Cleary  \n   *2020 - Eur. J. Mech./B Fluids, 84, 93-109*\n\n10. **[Benchmark of three-dimensional numerical models of subduction against a laboratory experiment](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.PEPI.2018.07.009)**  \n   Meriaux, C.A., D.A. May, J. Mansour, Z. Chen and O. Kaluza  \n   *2018 - Phys. Earth. Planet. Int., 283, 110-121*\n\n11. **[What can we learn from large bodies moving in a turbulent fluid?](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.EUROMECHFLU.2018.06.003)**  \n   Monaghan, J.J. and Meriaux, C.A  \n   *2018 - Eur. J. Mech. - B/Fluids, 72, 519-530.*\n\n12. **[An SPH study of turbulence near a free surface under gravity](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.EUROMECHFLU.2017.12.008)**  \n   Monaghan, J.J. and Mériaux, C.A.  \n   *2018 - Eur. J. Mech. - B/Fluids, 68, 201-210*\n\n13. **[A study of gravity currents carrying polydisperse particles along a V-shaped valley](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.EUROMECHFLU.2016.12.010)**  \n   Meriaux, C.A. and C.B. Kurz-Besson  \n   *2017 - Eur. J. Mech. - B/Fluids, 13, 0-13*\n\n14. **[Mantle plumes in the vicinity of subduction zones](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.EPSL.2016.09.001)**  \n   Meriaux, C.A., A-S. Meriaux, W. Schellart, J. Duarte, S.S. Duarte, and Z. Chen  \n   *2016 - Earth Planet. Sci. Lett., 454, 166-177*\n\n15. **[The combined effect of the minimum temperature increase and precipitation changes on Pinus pinaster in southern Portugal](https://doi.org/10.3389/FPLS.2016.01170)**  \n   Kurz Besson, C., J. Lousada, M. Gaspar, I. Correia, T. David, P. Soares, R. Cardoso, A. Russo, F. Varino, C. Meriaux, R. Trigo, and C. Gouveia  \n   *2016 - Front. Plant Sci., 7, 1170*\n\n16. **[Benchmarking analogue models of brittle thrust wedges](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.JSG.2016.03.005)**  \n   Schreurs, G., and Geomod 2008 group  \n   *2016 - J. Struct. Geology, 92, 116-139*\n\n17. **[The propagation of particulate gravity currents in a V-shaped triangular cross-section channel: experiments and shallow-water numerical simulations](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.4942241)**  \n   Meriaux, C.A., T. Zemach, C.B. Kurz-Besson, and M. Ungarish,  \n   *2016 - Phys. Fluids, 28, 036601.*\n\n18. **[A two-way interaction between the Hainan plume and the Manila subduction zone](https://doi.org/10.1002/2015GL064313)**  \n   Meriaux, C.A., J.C. Duarte, W.P. Schellart, and A-S. Meriaux  \n   *2015 - Geophys. Res. Lett., 42, 5796-5802.*\n\n19. **[Capture of the Canary mantle plume material by the Gibraltar arc mantle wedge during slab rollback](https://doi.org/10.1093/GJI/GGV120)**  \n   Mériaux, C.A., J.C. Duarte, S.S. Duarte, W. P. Schellart, Z. Chen, F. Rosas, J. Mata, P. Terrinha  \n   *2015 - Geophys. J. Int., 201 (3), 1717-1721.*\n\n20. **[The propagation of gravity currents in a V-shaped triangular cross-section channel: experiments and theory](https://doi.org/10.1017/JFM.2014.396)**  \n   Ungarish, M., C.A. Meriaux, and C.B. Kurz-Besson  \n   *2014 - J. Fluid Mech., 754, 232-249*\n\n21. **[Cork oak physiological responses to manipulated water availability in a Mediterranean woodland](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.AGRFORMET.2013.10.004)**  \n   Kurz-Besson C., R. Lobo do Vale, L. Rodrigues, P. Almeida, A. Herd, O.M. Grant, T.S. David, M. Schmidt, D. Otieno, T. Keenan, C. Gouveia, C. Mériaux, M.M. Chaves, J.S. Pereira  \n   *2014 - J. Agric. Meteorol., 184, 230-242*\n\n22. **[Sedimentation from binary suspensions in a turbulent gravity current along a V-shaped valley](https://doi.org/10.1017/JFM.2012.389)**  \n   Meriaux, C.A. and C.B. Kurz-Besson  \n   *2012 - J. Fluid Mech., 712, 624-645*\n\n23. **[Palaeomagnetic study of a sub-aerial volcanic ridge Sao Jorge Island, Azores) for the past 1.3 Myr: evidences for the Cobb Mountain Subchron, volcano flank instability and tectono-magmatic implications](https://doi.org/10.1111/J.1365-246X.2011.05320.X)**  \n   Silva, P.F, B. Henry, F.O. Marques, A. Hildenbrand, P. Madureira, C.A. Mériaux, and Z. Kratinova  \n   *2012 - Geophys. J. Int., 188 (3), 959-978*\n\n24. **[On the rise of strongly tilted mantle plume tails](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.PEPI.2010.10.013)**  \n   Meriaux, C., J.A. Mansour, L.N. Moresi, R.C. Kerr and D.A. May  \n   *2011 - Phys. Earth Planet. Int., 184 (1-2).*\n\n25. **[Rate Effects in dense granular materials: linear stability analysis and the fall of granular columns](https://doi.org/10.1002/NAG.895)**  \n   Lemiale, V., H.B. Muhlhaus, C. Meriaux, L. Moresi and L. Hudkinson  \n   *2011 - Int. J. Num. Anal. Meth. Geomech., 35, 293-308.*\n\n26. **[Particulate gravity currents along V-shaped valleys- ERRATUM](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0022112009991595)**  \n   Monaghan, J.J., C. Meriaux, H. Huppert and J. Mansour  \n   *2009 - J. Fluid Mech., 637, 475-475*\n\n27. **[Particulate gravity currents along V-shaped valleys](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0022112009007174)**  \n   Monaghan, J.J., C. Mériaux, H. Huppert and J. Mansour  \n   *2009 - J. Fluid Mech., 631, 419-440*\n\n28. **[Quasi-static fall of planar granular columns: comparison of 2D and 3D discrete element modelling with laboratory experiments](https://doi.org/10.1080/17486020902767388)**  \n   Owen, P.J., P.W. Cleary and C. Meriaux  \n   *2009 - Geomech. Geoengin., 4 (1), 55-77*\n\n29. **[High-Reynolds-number gravity currents along V-shaped valleys](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.EUROMECHFLU.2009.05.005)**  \n   Monaghan, J.J., C. Meriaux, H. Huppert and J.M. Monaghan  \n   *2009 - Eur. J. Mech. - B/Fluids, 28 (5), 651-659*\n\n30. **[Scaling the final deposits of cohesive granular columns after collapse and quasi-static fall](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.2870148)**  \n   Meriaux, C. and T. Triantafillou  \n   *2008 - Phys. Fluids, 20, 033301*\n\n31. **[Effect of thermal diffusion on the stability of strongly tilted mantle plume tails](https://doi.org/10.1029/2007JB005510)**  \n   Kerr R., C. Mériaux and J.R. Lister,  \n   *2008 - J. Geophys. Res., 113, B09401*\n\n32. **[Computational approaches to studying non-linear dynamics of the crust and mantle](https://doi.org/10.1016/J.PEPI.2007.06.009)**  \n   Moresi, L., S. Quenette, V. Lemiale, C. Mériaux, B. Appelbe and H.B. Muhlhaus  \n   *2007 - Phys. Earth Planet. Int., 163, 69-82*\n\n33. **[2D fall of granular columns controlled by slow horizontal withdrawal of a retaining wall](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.2335477)**  \n   Meriaux, C.  \n   *2006 - Phys. Fluids, 18, 093301*\n\n34. **[The structure of sheared viscous thermal plumes: implications for geochemical heterogeneities](https://doi.org/10.1029/2004GC000749)**  \n   Kerr, R.C. and C. Meriaux  \n   *2004 - Geochem. Geophys. Geosyst. 5, Q12009*\n\n35. **[Calculation of dike trajectories from volcanic centers](https://doi.org/10.1029/2001JB000436)**  \n   Meriaux, C. and J. R. Lister  \n   *2002 - J. Geophys. Res., 107, 2077-2087.*\n\n36. **[Dyke propagation with distributed damage of the host rock](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0012-821X(98)00264-7)**  \n   Meriaux, C., J. R. Lister, V. Lyakhovsky and A. Agnon  \n   *1999 - Earth Planet. Sci. Lett., 165, 177-185.*\n\n37. **[The thermal signature of subducted lithospheric slabs at the core-mantle boundary](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0012-821X(98)00110-1)**  \n   Meriaux, C., A. Agnon and J. R. Lister  \n   *1998 - Earth Planet. Sci. Lett., 160, 551-562*\n\n38. **[Dike propagation through an elastic plate](https://doi.org/10.1029/98JB00905)**  \n   Meriaux, C. and C. Jaupart  \n   *1998 - J. Geophys. Res., 103, 18,295-18,314.*\n\n39. **[Simple Fluid Dynamical Models of Volcanic Rift Zones](https://doi.org/10.1016/0012-821X(95)00170-H)**  \n   Meriaux, C. and C. Jaupart  \n   *1995 - Earth Planet. Sci. Lett., 136, 223-240.*\n\n40. **[Nonlinear Magnetooptics. The Photoinduced Faraday Rotation in Diluted Magnetic Semiconductors.](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-011-2536-9_6)**  \n   Frey, R., J. Frey, C. Meriaux and C. Flytzanis,  \n   *1992 - In: Ostrowsky D.B., Reinisch R. (eds) Guided Wave Nonlinear Optics. NATO ASI Series (Series E: Applied Sciences), vol 214. Springer, Dordrecht*"]],
